-- title:
--   Bijectivity of the dictionary Xi at Artinian local points
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison between rigidified fake elliptic curves and points of an abstract moduli package, and the assertion is that the comparison map $\Xi$ is bijective over an Artinian local base with algebraically closed residue field.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$ (`hrr`), and a non-zero natural number $N$ with $r \nmid N$ (`hrN`). A domain $\mathcal{O}$ of characteristic zero which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), completeness of $\mathcal{O}$ for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal{O}$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Next, a domain $Onr$ of characteristic zero, an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, subject to: $\pi$-adic completeness (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \bmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`); thus $Onr$ plays the role of the completion of the maximal unramified extension of $\mathcal{O}$, with $Fr$ lifting the Frobenius.
--
--   **Quaternionic data.** Rationals $a,b$ with `hB : IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`, i.e. $a > 0$ or $b > 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ the completion $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra precisely when $v$ divides $r$ or $\bar r$. A $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is an order maximal among orders (`hΛ`) and contains all rational integers (`hΛℤ`), and a map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to Z_{r}^{2} \times Z_{r}^{2}$, where $Z_{r}^{2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{r^{2}})$, satisfying `hcoord : IsOrderCoord Λ r coord`: additivity, normalisation $\mathrm{coord}(1) = (1,0)$, the twisted multiplication rule $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (m_1 m'_1 + r\, m_2 \sigma(m'_2),\; m_1 m'_2 + m_2 \sigma(m'_1))$ with $\sigma$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, injectivity, density of the image modulo every power of $r$, and the trace condition that $m + \bar m = n$ implies $m_1 + \sigma(m_1) = n$.
--
--   **The reduction data.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ of type $(\Lambda, N)$ over $Onr/(\pi)$ — an abelian scheme with commutative relative group law, relative fibre dimension two, a $\Lambda$-action compatible with the group law and the trace condition, and level data — a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ over $Onr/(\pi)$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`: multiplication by $r$ has kernel of degree $r^{4}$), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, and `hA₀ : A₀.IsFormalModuleVia coord X₀ θ₀`, asserting that $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with formal group law $X_0.F$ and that the $\Lambda$-action is given in these coordinates by $\mathrm{addVia}\,(X_0.\mathrm{act}\,\mathrm{coord}(m)_1)\,((X_0.\mathrm{act}\,\mathrm{coord}(m)_2)\circ X_0.\varpi)$.
--
--   **The special formal module and the moduli package.** A ring homomorphism $\iota : Z_r^{2} \to Onr$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ (`hΦ`: the two Lie summands are complementary and invertible) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`). A moduli package $M$ over $Onr$ — a functor $B \mapsto M.\mathrm{obj}\,B\,\psi$ on rings with a homomorphism from $Onr$ and $r$ nilpotent — which is a Zariski sheaf (`hM`), and a family of maps $\eta$ from rigidified objects $\mathrm{Rigidified}\,r\,\Phi\,B$ (a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X$ over $B$, an integer $n$, and a system $\rho$ of two power series over $B/(r)$) to $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B\,\psi$. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: for Noetherian $B$, two admissible rigidified objects (in the sense of `IsAdmissible ι ψ`: $X$ special, of height $4$, and $\rho$ an isogeny from $\Phi$ reduced to $X$ reduced of height $4n$) have the same image under $\eta$ if and only if they are isomorphic in the sense of `IsIsomorphic`; $\eta$ is natural for ring homomorphisms compatible with the $Onr$-structure, on admissible objects; and every point of $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B\,\psi$ is, after localisation away from each of finitely many elements generating the unit ideal, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$.
--
--   **Commensurability of the endomorphism centraliser.** A ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}(a)\}_a \cup \{\Phi.\varpi\mathrm{End}\}$ into $M_2(K_0)$ with `hE₀`: $E_0$ is injective, and there is $m$ such that every matrix in $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ becomes $r^{m}$ times a value of $E_0$, and $r^{m} E_0(e)$ lies in $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ for every $e$; so the image of $E_0$ is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal{O})$.
--
--   **Comparison of the two reductions.** A ring homomorphism $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a system of series $\beta_0$ over $Onr/(r)$ with `hβ₀`: $\beta_0$ is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ pushed forward along $\kappa$, of height $4n_0$.
--
--   **The dictionary.** A family $\Xi$ assigning, to each Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$, each $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$ and each proof that $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B$, a map from $\mathrm{RigidifiedCurve}\,r\,\pi\,A_0\,B\,\psi$ — pairs consisting of a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$ together with a rigidification: a curve $E_b$ over $B/(\pi)$ pulled back from $E$, a curve $A_b$ over $B/(\pi)$ pulled back from $A_0$ along the residue leg of $\psi$, an integer $d$, and a pair $\varphi, \varphi'$ forming an $r^{d}$-isogeny pair between $E_b$ and $A_b$ with $\varphi$ preserving level — to the $G$-points $\mathrm{GPoint}\,\mathcal{O}\,M\,B$, that is triples consisting of a leg $\psi$, nilpotence of $r$, and a point of $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B\,\psi$. Four axioms are imposed on $\Xi$:
--
--   `hΞleg`: over a base $B$ in which every idempotent is $0$ or $1$, the leg of $\Xi\,x$ is $\psi \circ Fr^{k}$ for some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   `hΞnat`: for an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\varphi : B \to B'$ between Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent in both, and objects $x$ over $(B,\psi)$, $x'$ over $(B', \varphi \circ \psi)$ such that $g$ exhibits the curve of $x'$ as the pullback of that of $x$ along $\varphi$ and the rigidifications correspond via $g$, one has $\Xi\,x' = (\Xi\,x).\mathrm{map}\,\varphi$.
--
--   `hΞiso`: if $i$ is an isomorphism of the underlying curves of $x$ and $x'$ over $B$ compatible with the structural morphisms and with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action and the level (`IsoVia`), and if in addition there exist $i_b$ on the reductions with $i_b$ followed by $x'_{,2}.g_b$ equal to $x_{,2}.g_b$ followed by $i$ and $i_b$ followed by $x'_{,2}.E_b.f$ equal to $x_{,2}.E_b.f$, a morphism $u_A$ exhibiting $x'_{,2}.A_b$ as a pullback of $x_{,2}.A_b$ along the identity with $u_A$ followed by $x_{,2}.g_A$ equal to $x'_{,2}.g_A$, and natural numbers $i_1, j_1$ making the composite $i_b$, $x'_{,2}.\varphi$, $u_A$, $x_{,2}.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{i_1})$ equal to the composite $x_{,2}.\varphi$, $x_{,2}.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{j_1})$, then $\Xi\,x = \Xi\,x'$.
--
--   `hΞdef`: over a Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ with only trivial idempotents, with $\pi$ and $r$ nilpotent, and for $x$ carrying a formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ via $\mathrm{coord}$, there are $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ over $B$ with $t.X = X$, with the rigidification of $x$ transported to $t$ by $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$ and the $r^{j}$-power substitution (`IsRigTransport`), with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the twisted leg $\psi \circ Fr^{-j}$, and with $\Xi\,x$ equal to the $G$-point with leg $\psi \circ Fr^{-j}$ and point $\eta(t)$.
--
--   **Base.** Finally $B$ is an Artinian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra whose residue field is algebraically closed, and $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B$ (`hB`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Two assertions hold.
--
--   First (surjectivity, across all legs): for every $y \in \mathrm{GPoint}\,\mathcal{O}\,M\,B$ there exist an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$ and an object $x \in \mathrm{RigidifiedCurve}\,r\,\pi\,A_0\,B\,\psi$ with $\Xi\,B\,\psi\,hB\,x = y$.
--
--   Second (injectivity within a fixed leg, up to the equivalence appearing in `hΞiso`): for every $\psi$ and all $x, x' \in \mathrm{RigidifiedCurve}\,r\,\pi\,A_0\,B\,\psi$ with $\Xi\,B\,\psi\,hB\,x = \Xi\,B\,\psi\,hB\,x'$, there exist an isomorphism $i : x_{,1}.A \cong x'_{,1}.A$ with $i$ followed by $x'_{,1}.f$ equal to $x_{,1}.f$ such that `FakeEllipticCurve.IsoVia` holds for $i$ (compatibility with the relative group law, with the $\Lambda$-action, and with factorisation through the level scheme), together with a morphism $i_b : x_{,2}.E_b.A \to x'_{,2}.E_b.A$ satisfying: $i_b$ followed by $x'_{,2}.g_b$ equals $x_{,2}.g_b$ followed by $i$, and $i_b$ followed by $x'_{,2}.E_b.f$ equals $x_{,2}.E_b.f$; a morphism $u_A : x'_{,2}.A_b.A \to x_{,2}.A_b.A$ exhibiting $x'_{,2}.A_b$ as the pullback of $x_{,2}.A_b$ along the identity ring homomorphism, with $u_A$ followed by $x_{,2}.g_A$ equal to $x'_{,2}.g_A$; and natural numbers $i_1, j_1$ such that the composite of $i_b$, $x'_{,2}.\varphi$, $u_A$ and $x_{,2}.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{i_1})$ equals the composite of $x_{,2}.\varphi$ and $x_{,2}.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{j_1})$. Thus the two rigidified curves are isomorphic with their rigidifications matching up to $r$-power multiplications, which is the converse of the implication assumed in `hΞiso`.
--
--   This is the Artinian-point form of the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary: over an Artinian local base with algebraically closed residue field and $\pi$ nilpotent, the comparison map $\Xi$ from rigidified fake elliptic curves to points of the moduli package is surjective (legs being allowed to move by powers of the Frobenius $Fr$) and injective on each fixed leg up to isomorphism of curves matching the rigidifications up to $r$-power scalars. It feeds the construction of the formal uniformisation of the Shimura curve at $r$, being used by the statements on labelled lifts and on the bijectivity and rigidity of the $\Omega$-family.
